# 93_hui_electrocuted — PHOBOS AS THE ELECTROCUTE VICTIM (14z-95).
#
# TRIGGER, maintainer-supplied 2026-08-18 and NOT guessable from the corpus:
# Victor's HELD HP or 6+HP — "the electrocuting version of his HP". It is a
# CHARGEABLE NORMAL, not a special. Also supplied, and equally load-bearing:
# Donovan's 214+P electrocute does NOT produce the reported sound, so the
# trigger is Victor-specific rather than "any electrocute".
#
# WHAT THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS RIG GOT WRONG, kept because it is the trap:
# it fired 236+HP (D/DL/L3, five frames). That CONNECTED on both legs —
# identical HP 288->275 at f3416 — and produced an ordinary hit: victim
# classes 0x00/0x04/0x05, freeze 0x0a, and ZERO rows with the electric-shake
# signature. A short press is the QUICK version of the same normal. A rig
# that lands a hit and reports clean is the expensive kind of wrong, which is
# why the consumer asserts the shake installed before it compares any sound.
#
# ALSO MEASURED, and it retires a misleading name: tests/replays/
# 32_victor_shock_vsav2.rpl does NOT produce a shock on NATIVE vs2 either
# (classes 0x00/0x05, freeze 0x0b, no class 0x06). Its "victor_shock" name is
# aspirational. That corroborates rather than contradicts STATE 14z-74/14z-76,
# which record twice that no existing replay produces an electrocute.
#
# THREE ATTEMPTS, deliberately different, because which one fires decides
# what was captured:
#   f3400 held HP, 70 frames, NEUTRAL  — the charge version at walk-in range
#   f3700 6+HP held, 70 frames         — forward + HP, charged
#   f4000 6+HP quick, 5 frames         — the control: this is the version the
#                                        first rig fired, and it must NOT
#                                        install the shake
# P2 stands on the RIGHT, so his forward is LEFT (`L`); button 3 is HP, the
# same spelling replay 32 uses for Victor.
#
# POKES: the standard forced-pick pair, P1=0x10 Phobos (victim, $FF8400),
# P2=0x03 Victor (attacker, $FF8800). 0x03 is Victor on vsav AND vsav2, so
# one script serves both legs.
